{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; tail -n 62 docs/CURRENT_STATE.md; sed -n '10699,10790p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n\n> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n\n## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n\nBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n\n1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n   word for it is already given.\n4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n\n## 1. Goal (end to end)\n\nEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\nnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\nany one step's own score.\n\n## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n\n**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n\nThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n\n- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n  nothing at all and were never judged.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n| Evidence | Content | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n\nAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n\n## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n\n| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n\n## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n\n- **The frozen ruler** (AGENTS §7.1, D261, D262): the extraction standard, version 5 (the signed version 3 with D248's seven boundaries and D250's four\n  evidence boundaries and counting unit), golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold; not a word changes; a disagreement is a gap in the\n  system (D263).\n- **The frozen extraction prompt** (D274): v74i (`poc-v108`) does not change by a word; what this phase changes is the one place that holds the filing\n  rule (the description of the `links` field), made into a new version with a new configuration and compared with v74i. Alex has given his word for this\n  work (2026-11-04: \"好你去解决问题\", \"开始做\"); making it the default needs his word again.\n- **Frozen opening and judging** (D354): this phase does not touch them.\n- **The standard's text** (in translation): §6, \"file under the narrowest open question; when no question takes it, record no home and drop it, and read\n  each one at evaluation\"; §5.1, \"makes the answer to some question more or less likely\", with four paths, \"demand, supply, competition, pricing\"; §5.3,\n  \"another company's matter is not kept when one cannot say which question about the covered company it moves through a path such as demand, supply,\n  competition or pricing\".\n- **Decisions**: D207 (one home per fact, the narrowest question), D209 (no home means dropped, each one read), D259 (leeway: a figure question takes\n  \"the demand, supply, competition and pricing that make it more or less likely\"; the total-revenue question taking more is an accepted cost, \"bounded by\n  the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\"), D265 (code keeps only the narrowest), D273 (the fallback sentence), D355 (the two failed tests\n  of the previous filing topic).\n- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n\n## 5. What to do\n\nStatus: the links-rule version that carried items 1–3 (v74l) failed its one test (§8 step 5); D361 keeps the fallback sentence, so item 3 is not carried out. The plan below is kept as written.\n\n1. **Replace the abstract sentence \"makes the answer more or less likely through demand, supply, competition or pricing\" with the few classes in which an analyst recognises an indirect relation, written as a closed set, each class with two or three examples from other industries** (AGENTS §6: one\n   abstract sentence is a coin flip on every send; examples replace it). A first draft of the classes (checked against the standard line by line at\n   design time; it holds only meanings the standard already has):\n   - The question's own figure: this question's figures, guidance, estimates and revisions (estimates for another period or another population follow\n     the existing rule).\n   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n   maker's share question); when one sentence gives a separate figure for each of two questions, write two items, each filed under its own question\n   (code stores two items that quote the same sentence as one record linked to both questions); never also file it under a broader question about the\n   same company (the failure of D355's test 2: the model filed it under an NVIDIA revenue question as well). The original draft's \"when the other\n   party's question is open … file it there as well\" conflicted with D207 and was deleted on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement (D360).\n3. **Delete the fallback sentence** and return to the standard's §6: when no question takes a fact, it is not filed; code drops it as \"no home\" and\n   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n\n## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n\n- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n  measured and reported apart.\n- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n  counted after.\n- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n    question\";\n  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n    or no question uses);\n  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n    ones that matter one by one.\n- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n      facts must not fall.\n  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n      what was measured.\n  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n\n## 7. Process (no drift)\n\n- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n  sentence, in both directions.\n- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n\n## 8. Steps and where we are\n\n| # | Step | Output | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n\n## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n\n- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n- **The slice (D450) ran and the wiring works live** (`clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/SLICE-RESULT.md`): both draws complete in 8\n  minutes, every component with real sends on one ledger, every ledger line attributed; $0.159 of ledger (about $0.15 at the published\n  rates). Every answer read.\n- **The full run (D451) completed** (`poc/data/clean-e2e-full-2026-11-13/`, 05:44–08:33; c2's Step 1 redone after a dropped response,\n  D453): ledger $11.43, every line attributed (`spend-by-component.json`). Clean 15/10/15 theses, 69/72/92 judgements; production 12/13/14\n  views, 92/80/104 judgements, 0 failed. First read in the round log §84.\n- **The full run, read (D452–D455):** Stage A on the 40 fresh theses misses lines 1–5 and meets line 6 (`STAGE-A-FULL-RESULT.md`); the\n  census (`CENSUS-FULL-RESULT.md`): settled agreements on the seven graded questions clean 2/1/0, production 1/1/1 (2/2/1 and 2/2/2 with\n  unresolved points counted), no eligible judgement disagrees, every loss at opening; the clean path kept 51 of 55 decisive facts of its lost\n  questions, production 39 and left kept Kyber and Ohio facts unlinked; Stage B line 3 unexercised (0 settled, at most 4 of 8), its judgement\n  reading stopped (D455).\n- **Traced since (D456–D458), all at $0:** production's kept-but-unlinked facts cost no final answer in this window (three mechanisms,\n  `unlinked-trace/UNLINKED-TRACE-RESULT.md`); the opening losses hold few shown breaches of the standard, spread over small mechanisms, and\n  their largest classes are choices the standard leaves open (`opening-review/OPENING-REVIEW.md`); no opening phase is opened.\n- **Since (D458–D460):** Stage A's first-pass faults form no class (`stage-a-trace/STAGE-A-TRACE-RESULT.md`); the model's accounts make\n  two opening mechanisms strong leads, the unsigned Ohio guarantee folded into the programme's amount (seven draws, both systems) and the\n  next year's capex folded into this year's question (five of six clean draws), and show the standard's keep-apart line for unsigned deals\n  missing from the clean Step 1 request (`opening-review/D459-RESULT.md`).\n- **Since (D460–D462):** the whole-request audit of clean Step 1 (two readers apart, `step1-audit/AUDIT.md`) finds the standard's identity\n  boundaries missing from its request (the unsigned deal beside a plan's amount; another year of a shared quantity) and one date sentence\n  narrower than the standard (F6); F6 decided only one or two of 26 memory declines (`step1-audit/f6/`) and is recorded, not tested alone.\n  The status report for Alex, reviewed by the council for accuracy: `clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/STATUS-FOR-ALEX-2026-11-13.md`.\n- **Since (D463–D464):** the identity lever is a prompt clause first; candidate c16 (version 5, four blind audits) and a case-specific\n  diagnostic on fresh articles (one story per target boundary is all this window holds), 7 merge and 9 negative stories and the stored guards\n  (`clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/PROBE-DESIGN-v2.md`).\n- **Since (D465–D466):** the identity diagnostic moved its error (c16 not adopted, c8 stays; `identity-probe/v2/RESULT.md`, $4.49); a deep\n  reflection council (Alex's request) agreed the second half of the day circled and reset the plan.\n- **Since (D467–D473):** the ladder-cost proofs are kept as tools (`ladder-cost/`); the ladder-refusal candidate stopped on a contested\n  \"main figure\" boundary; the 41 Stage A reader splits recorded, none changing its verdict; the ten right-by-refusal theses traced; the period\n  candidate c17 failed its frozen lines (memory and federal openings suppressed; `period-probe/RESULT.md`). Step 1 sentence changes on these\n  articles stop (D473). Provisional choices under D439 (D469): empty-start measurement; the three open shapes stay concerns; the written-ladder\n  line stands (D471). Alex's answer overrides any of them.\n- **Since (D474–D477):** the unsigned-deal candidate c18 was not adopted (`unsigned-deals/RESULT.md`). The source check of the eleven\n  first-pass refusals (`reachability/RESULT.md`): six right, four reachable only on paper, one split. **D477:** Stage A not passed; no theme or\n  ladder change for the H100 boundary; no more Step 1 sentence changes on these articles; production stays the default; the clean path's\n  three product choices are written for Alex (`clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/CHOICES-FOR-ALEX-2026-11-13.md`).\n- **Since (D478–D482), under D439:** choice 2 tested and failed (c19); choice 3 taken (production stays the default); production's price\n  rule (D191.4) tested and failed (`docs/research/2026-11-13-shared-quantity-price/`); no further paid test on the frozen ruler (D482).\n- **Alex active again (2026-11-14).** D483–D486: a use-oriented acceptance was designed and its $0 trial read\n  (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/TRIAL-RESULT.md`); Alex asked for a leaner path (small steps, each run fast). D487: step 1 built nothing (the\n  \"dated after the article\" statements are correct dates against items stored a day early); step 2 runs one fresh week (2026-09-22..28, 235\n  items proved identical for both systems), one draw a system, a one-day slice first, readings A and B, the decision rule written before\n  reading (`LEAN-LOG.md` §2). Slice passed; production's week draw complete; the clean draw stopped twice on dropped connections, root-caused and fixed (D488: Step 1 resends a\n  network loss, each attempt on the ledger). The clean week draw completed from empty (`poc/data/clean-lean-week-2026-11-14/week-r2/`, $2.52; both ledgers $4.69). **Result (D489,\n  `docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/WEEK-RESULT.md`):** not clear by the rule written before reading; no system chosen. Production\n  covers 4 of the 5 big matters two blind reader families listed, the clean path 2 (it lacks revenue and margin by D382/D384); judgement\n  support does not rank them at this size. The question then put to Alex (whether the new system holds questions on the company's main\n  numbers) was answered by him on 2026-11-15 (D505; see \"One next task\").\n  Recorded spend under D439 at that point: about $18.4 of $40 (about $7.26 before the run; the run's ledger $11.43, somewhat above its cost at the\n  published rates); the current figure is in \"One next task\". (An earlier line here gave \"about $29.2\" as tracked spend; no decision or\n  log records that figure and it is withdrawn.)\n- **Open defects recorded by this review:** the frozen judge retried 10, 16 and 18 judgements in the seeded clean draws, 38 of 51\n  reasons a theme cited as a dependency (PRD 11, E4), and its stored path holds the 2026-07-05 fault above (frozen; findings for\n  Alex); the same matter takes different shapes between draws (Kyber due end-2027 in one draw and end-2028 in another); under D397\n  the second bet of a topic that holds two has no answer; five-state event ladders failed by hand three times before a construction now in the request\n  produced ones that pass a reader; in Stage A the model's own are faulted by both reader families in 10 of 17 for the company.\n- **The unseeded measurement (D395):** graded subset 4.0 against 6.33 seeded. Every miss is a question not opened in the ruler's shape.\n  Judged answers 16 of 16, on the ruler's wording and ladder; the system's own questions have never met a judge.\n- **Stage 2 of D392:** clean 18, 18, 17 against v108 17, 15, 16, inconclusive on q12.\n- **Known gap (D394):** capital spending (q12).\n\nNo long job is running.\n## D713 · 2026-11-21 · Council R passes the citation-only judge design (round 38, four passes); build judging-v12 and poc-v121 with the $0 test\n\n- **Design:** `docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/DESIGN.md` v3.\n  - `judging-v12` keeps judging-v7's text and request; its four id fields take closed lists built from the rows that render the\n    request (one shared `$defs` evidence enum, plain dependency and theme enums, `maxItems: 0` for an empty list).\n  - Under v12, `check_answer` uses the same lists.\n  - `model.check_schema` gains `maxItems`.\n  - Preflight checks judge rows against their saved format and fails on a judge row without one.\n  - `poc-v121` = poc-v118 with judging-v12. No v8-based version.\n  - No pre-send size rule: an HTTP 400 is the visible stop.\n- **Council** (`codex-r38.md` … `codex-r38d.md`, `claude-r38.md`), one round each:\n  - 38, NOT READY: the format-error path; preflight; the D358 sizes (the sealed run's 647-id request needs 1,323 plain enum values);\n    lists from the rows, not parsed text; the name `judging-v9` already used; T1's mechanics.\n  - 38b, NOT READY: two owners of membership (a footnote in a quote makes the text parser narrow the list); preflight for judge rows\n    only; the v7/v8 prompt hook; T1's assertions.\n  - 38c, NOT READY: T1 must compare the lists' ids; a real v12 retry test; preflight must fail on a missing format.\n  - 38d: READY TO BUILD.\n- **Also recorded:** the same id-membership class halted the sealed three-month run (D358, 2026-10-28). The v7/v8 text parser can\n  narrow its allowed set (a finding; frozen versions unchanged).\n- **Next:** the build, its tests and mutants, and the $0 test T1–T5 as registered in DESIGN.md §5. Any paid step (provider acceptance;\n  whether the judge picks the intended source) asks Alex again (D712).\n\n## D714 · 2026-11-21 · judging-v12 and poc-v121 built; every registered $0 check passes; the paid acceptance step is Alex's\n\n- **Built (uncommitted):**\n  - `judging.py`: v12, its closed format, `request_parts`, `check_answer`'s lists, and the round's per-view format;\n  - `model.check_schema`: `maxItems`;\n  - `prompts/judging-v12.txt`: a v7 copy;\n  - `poc/config/poc-v121.json`;\n  - preflight: part A on saved judge formats, and an exit on a missing one;\n  - tests: `tests/test_citation_lists.py` (7) and the frozen pins.\n- **Checks:** suite 388 passed (the frozen tests included); mutants 12 of 12 caught; `docs/check.py` OK.\n- **The $0 test** (`docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/RESULT.md`):\n  - **T1:** old-2 replayed under poc-v121. 1,616 judge responses, 4 labelled substitutions, 0 request or list problems; the store\n    equals old-2's on all 36 shared tables, apart from the named columns.\n  - **T1b:** the four refused originals raise `SchemaError` on their bad ids.\n  - **T1c, T3, T3b, T5:** pass.\n  - **T2:** 5,408 distinct pairs; all 5,391 valid answers pass; all 17 refused fail on their bad id (e362, e3111 among them).\n  - **T4:** the largest request in the five archives is 464 enum values counted once (915 per reference); in the sealed D358 run, 676\n    (1,323 per reference).\n  - **Format tokens** (an o200k_base estimate): v7 315; v12 median 502, max 2,621.\n- **Deviations, disclosed in RESULT.md:**\n  - T1's first comparison crashed (FTS tables have no rowid). Three differences were named only after they were seen: a timestamp, an\n    empty new table, a new column on an empty table. T1 was rerun in full.\n  - Run 2's summary print failed after its passing results were saved; the print is fixed.\n  - Preflight's malformed-row ordering was found by council round 39 and fixed, with a mutant.\n  - A mistaken `t1_replay.py --help` started a stray replay into `./--help`. It was stopped, the folder was moved to the session\n    scratchpad, and the script now refuses an option as its folder.\n- **Council round 39** (`codex-r39.md`): the build matches DESIGN v3, no v7/v8 regression; its findings taken.\n- **Not shown** (DESIGN §6):\n  - provider acceptance of the format and its counting of a referenced enum;\n  - whether the judge, constrained, cites the intended source;\n  - whether forecasts move.\n\n  A paid step answers these, and it is Alex's (D712): acceptance on the largest and an empty-list request, then the 17 refused\n  requests and a matched set of accepted ones under v12, read beside their sources.\n\n## D715 · 2026-11-22 · Alex approves $0.50 for the paid acceptance step of judging-v12\n\n- **The question:** \"引用格式的新版本已经做好，所有不花钱的验证都通过了。要不要花一点钱实测？实测这样做：先发最大的一次请求和一次名单为空的请求，确认服务商接受这个格式；再把当初被退回的 17 个请求和 17 个正常请求用新版本重发，人工对照原文看模型有没有选对证据。每个请求发一次，约 0.10 美元；各发三次看稳不稳，约 0.30 美元。发之前我会先和审查员定好怎么读、怎么判。\"\n  Options: 0.5 美元 (recommended: three sends per request with a margin; a stop above $0.50; the reading rule agreed with the council\n  before the send; Claude reads the results); 0.15 美元 (one send per request); 先不花 (stop; poc-v118 stays the product).\n- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22): \"0.5 美元 (推荐)\".\n- **Next:** the probe design and its reading rule through the council at $0, the harness dry-run at $0, then the send under a $0.50\n  reservation ledger.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/PROBE-DESIGN.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe_plan.py; ls -l docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe-plan.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/PROBE-DESIGN.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe_plan.py; ls -l docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe-plan.json'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Paid acceptance probe of judging-v12 (design, D715; registered before any send)\n     2\t\n     3\t**Alex's ceiling:** $0.50 (D715, \"0.5 美元 (推荐)\"), held by a reservation ledger (`budget_replay.SharedLedger`) that stops at it.\n     4\t**Plan file:** `probe-plan.json` (`probe_plan.py`, $0). **Model and settings:** poc-v121's rejudging setting (gpt-6-luna, medium\n     5\teffort, 16,000 output tokens), the provider's strict format.\n     6\t\n     7\t## 1. What it decides\n     8\t\n     9\t| Question | How it is answered |\n    10\t|---|---|\n    11\t| Q1. Does the provider accept judging-v12's format on a real request, the largest one included? | Part A |\n    12\t| Q2. On the requests the open format let the model get wrong, does it now cite the intended source, or put a wrong but listed id in its place? | Part B |\n    13\t| Q3. On ordinary requests, does the closed format change what the judge cites or concludes, beyond its own variation under the open format? | Part C |\n    14\t\n    15\tPart A also records whether the provider counts a referenced enum once. That decides whether three-month runs fit; it is not a gate.\n    16\t\n    17\t## 2. The sends\n    18\t\n    19\tEach request is sent with its stored system and user text, unchanged.\n    20\t\n    21\t- **Part A, acceptance, 2 sends.**\n    22\t  - (A1) the largest stored request of the five archives: old-1 call 5652, 451 evidence ids; 464 enum values counted once, 915 per\n    23\t    reference;\n    24\t  - (A2) the sealed D358 run's largest: call 6918, 647 ids; 676 counted once, 1,323 per reference.\n    25\t  - Every request also carries `cites_views` with `maxItems: 0` (no dependency in poc-v118), so Part A exercises the empty-list form.\n    26\t  - A2 is a format check on an older request text (poc-v34's judge request with v12's format built from its lists). Its answer is\n    27\t    not read.\n    28\t- **Part B, the refused requests: 16 distinct, 3 sends each, under v12's format.**\n    29\t  - 17 refused rows give 16 distinct requests: control-1's 8941 is 8938's retry (the same request plus the note).\n    30\t  - linked-1's 7 requests carry judging-v8's dependency block. They are sent with v12's builder's format for their lists, a format\n    31\t    probe on the stored text. No v8-based version exists or is built.\n    32\t- **Part C, matched ordinary requests: 16 distinct, 3 sends under v12's format and 3 under judging-v7's open format** (the fresh\n    33\t  control). Each matched request is the same question's accepted first-attempt request in the same archive with the closest evidence\n    34\t  count (`probe_plan.py`).\n    35\t- **Order:** A, then B, then C with each request's v12 and v7 sends together and the cheapest requests first. A ceiling stop leaves\n    36\t  only complete pairs, and the report says which requests were not reached.\n    37\t- **Estimate** (stored tokens at the published rates): about $0.43. Actual output tokens vary; the ledger stops at $0.50.\n    38\t- **One send at a time per request,** a thread pool of 4 across requests.\n    39\t- **A transport or provider error** is recorded and not retried beyond the transport's own policy. An HTTP 400 is the Part A\n    40\t  finding.\n    41\t\n    42\t## 3. Reading rule (frozen before the send)\n    43\t\n    44\t### Part A\n    45\tQ1 passes when A1 returns HTTP 200 with an answer that passes the format and `check_answer` against its lists. For A2, a 200 means\n    46\ta referenced enum counts once (or the provider tolerates 1,323); a 400 is recorded with its body.\n    47\t\n    48\t### Part B\n    49\tEach of the 48 answers is read by hand beside its request.\n    50\t- **The fact.** First, before any answer is read, the original refused answer is read: for each bad id, the fact its reasons or push\n    51\t  attach to it, and the request's entry that carries that fact (by quote), or \"ambiguous\" when two entries could. These are the\n    52\t  intended targets, written to `probe-targets.json`.\n    53\t- **Evidence-id cases** (e88, e3955, e3998, e1382, e2769, e393, e362/e3111, e693). Each answer is classed for each target:\n    54\t  - *intended*: it cites the entry that carries the fact;\n    55\t  - *dropped*: no listed entry is cited for that fact;\n    56\t  - *misattributed*: it cites a listed entry for that fact whose quote does not carry it;\n    57\t  - *unclear*: the answer no longer states the fact. That counts as dropped and is reported apart.\n    58\t- **Placement cases** (theme ids in cites or `cites_views`; the dependency v14 as evidence). *Correct*: the theme appears only in\n    59\t  `theme_pushes`, or the dependency only in `cites_views`. *Absent*: it is not named.\n    60\t- **Every answer:** passes `check_answer` (a refusal is counted); its rung and implied outcome are set beside the stored accepted\n    61\t  retry's.\n    62\t- A second, blind reader (Codex) reads the same 48 against the frozen targets. A disagreement goes to a third, source-backed read.\n    63\t\n    64\t### Part C\n    65\tPer request: the rungs of the 3 v12 and the 3 v7 answers, and each answer's cited set against the stored answer's (the share of\n    66\tthe stored cites kept). A format effect is flagged when:\n    67\t- the v12 rungs fall outside the range of the v7 rungs and the stored rung; or\n    68\t- v12's mean share of kept cites is below v7's by more than v7's own spread (its max minus its min).\n    69\t\n    70\tEvery flagged request is read by hand: is a source dropped or changed, and why?\n    71\t\n    72\t### Outcome (registered)\n    73\t- **Ready to adopt** (to propose to Alex as the product's judge format), when all hold:\n    74\t  - Q1 passes;\n    75\t  - in Part B no answer is refused and misattributed answers are at most 2 of 48;\n    76\t  - in Part C no flagged request shows a source wrongly dropped or replaced because of the format.\n    77\t- **Not ready**, with its reason:\n    78\t  - Q1 fails;\n    79\t  - more than 2 misattributions;\n    80\t  - a Part B refusal;\n    81\t  - a Part C format effect confirmed by reading.\n    82\t\n    83\tUnclear or ambiguous cases are reported and do not count for or against. Both outcomes go to Alex; adoption itself is his decision.\n    84\t\n    85\t### Why 2 of 48\n    86\tThe open format turned these same requests into refusals 17 of 17 times. Under the closed format a misattribution passes silently,\n    87\tso it is the harm to bound. Two in 48 (about 4%) is the order of the original slips' rate on these hardest requests. A higher rate\n    88\twould trade a visible refusal for a silent wrong citation too often.\n    89\t\n    90\t## 4. Harness and $0 proof before the send\n    91\t\n    92\t`probe.py`, harness-only:\n    93\t- it reads `probe-plan.json`, builds each request with `model.build_request` from poc-v121's setting and v12's format (or v7's), and\n    94\t  sends through `budget_replay.BudgetTransport` on a ledger at $0.50;\n    95\t- each answer goes through `model.check_schema` and `judging.check_answer` (with the lists);\n    96\t- answers are written to `probe-answers.jsonl` with the request's sha, part, send number and cost.\n    97\t\n    98\tBefore the send, at $0:\n    99\t- a dry run with a fake transport answering stored answers checks that every request is built as planned: the system and user text\n   100\t  equal the stored ones; the format equals `schema_for('judging-v12', lists)`, or v7's for the control;\n   101\t- the ledger refuses a ceiling other than $0.50;\n   102\t- a mutant (a request sent with the open format in Part B) is caught by the dry check.\n     1\t\"\"\"$0: the paid acceptance probe's request plan (PROBE-DESIGN.md; D715). Writes probe-plan.json:\n     2\t\n     3\t- acceptance: the largest stored request of the five archives (most evidence ids), and the sealed D358 run's largest (647 ids; its\n     4\t  shared evidence enum is under the documented 1,000 values only if a referenced enum counts once);\n     5\t- refused: the 17 distinct stored refused judge requests (old-1, old-2, old-3, control-1 and linked-1 fresh rows), each with its bad ids;\n     6\t- matched: for each refused request, the same question's accepted first-attempt request in the same archive whose evidence count is\n     7\t  closest (ties: the nearest earlier call), never a request of the refused set.\n     8\t\n     9\tEach entry carries the stored system and user texts, the closed lists read from the text, and a cost estimate from the stored tokens at\n    10\tthe published gpt-6-luna rates.\n    11\t\n    12\t  python3 probe_plan.py\n    13\t\"\"\"\n    14\timport json\n    15\timport re\n    16\timport sqlite3\n    17\timport sys\n    18\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    19\t\n    20\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    21\tREPO = HERE.parents[2]\n    22\tsys.path.insert(0, str(REPO / 'src'))\n    23\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\n    24\tfrom hivemind import model                                              # noqa: E402\n    25\tfrom hivemind.views import judging                                     # noqa: E402\n    26\timport t2_offline                                                       # noqa: E402\n    27\t\n    28\tPRICE_IN, PRICE_OUT = model.PRICES['gpt-6-luna']\n    29\t\n    30\t\n    31\tdef view_of(user):\n    32\t    match = re.match(r'Thesis (v\\d+) \\(', user)\n    33\t    return match.group(1) if match else None\n    34\t\n    35\t\n    36\tdef cost(tokens_in, tokens_out):\n    37\t    return round((tokens_in or 0) / 1e6 * PRICE_IN + (tokens_out or 0) / 1e6 * PRICE_OUT, 6)\n    38\t\n    39\t\n    40\tdef entry(label, call_id, request, tokens_in, tokens_out, answer_json=None):\n    41\t    user = request['messages'][1]['content'].split(t2_offline.RETRY_MARK, 1)[0]\n    42\t    lists = t2_offline.lists_from_text(user)\n    43\t    return {'source': label, 'call_id': call_id, 'view': view_of(user), 'system': request['messages'][0]['content'], 'user': user,\n    44\t            'lists': lists, 'evidence count': len(lists['evidence']), 'stored answer': answer_json,\n    45\t            'estimated cost': cost(tokens_in, tokens_out), 'stored tokens': [tokens_in, tokens_out]}\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\tdef main():\n    49\t    refused, accepted_by_source, largest = [], {}, None\n    50\t    seen = set()\n    51\t    for label, folder, top in t2_offline.SOURCES:\n    52\t        archive = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{folder / \"archive.sqlite\"}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    53\t        views = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{folder / \"views.sqlite\"}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    54\t        committed = {r[0] for r in views.execute('select call_id from judgements')}\n    55\t        for call_id, sha, request_json, answer_json, tokens_in, tokens_out in archive.execute(\n    56\t                \"select call_id, request_sha256, request_json, answer_json, input_tokens, output_tokens from model_calls \"\n    57\t                \"where purpose='judge' and call_id>? order by call_id\", (top,)):\n    58\t            request = json.loads(request_json)\n    59\t            pair = (sha.split(':')[0], answer_json)\n    60\t            if pair in seen:\n    61\t                continue\n    62\t            seen.add(pair)\n    63\t            item = entry(label, call_id, request, tokens_in, tokens_out, answer_json)\n    64\t            if largest is None or item['evidence count'] > largest['evidence count']:\n    65\t                largest = item\n    66\t            if ':refused:' in sha:\n    67\t                schema = judging.schema_for('judging-v12', item['lists'])\n    68\t                item['bad ids'] = sorted({re.search(r\"'([^']+)' not in\", e).group(1) for e in model.check_schema(json.loads(answer_json), schema)\n    69\t                                          if \"' not in\" in e})\n    70\t                refused.append(item)\n    71\t            elif call_id in committed and t2_offline.RETRY_MARK not in request['messages'][1]['content']:\n    72\t                accepted_by_source.setdefault(label, []).append(item)\n    73\t    distinct = {}\n    74\t    for item in refused:                           # a retry carries its first request's text: one request, the bad ids of both attempts\n    75\t        key = (item['source'], item['user'])\n    76\t        if key in distinct:\n    77\t            distinct[key]['bad ids'] = sorted(set(distinct[key]['bad ids']) | set(item['bad ids']))\n    78\t            distinct[key]['refused calls'].append(item['call_id'])\n    79\t        else:\n    80\t            distinct[key] = dict(item, **{'refused calls': [item['call_id']]})\n    81\t    refused = list(distinct.values())\n    82\t    refused_users = set(distinct)\n    83\t    matched = []\n    84\t    for item in refused:\n    85\t        pool = [a for a in accepted_by_source.get(item['source'], []) if a['view'] == item['view'] and (a['source'], a['user']) not in refused_users]\n    86\t        if not pool:\n    87\t            raise SystemExit(f'{item[\"source\"]} {item[\"call_id\"]}: no accepted request of {item[\"view\"]} to match')\n    88\t        best = min(pool, key=lambda a: (abs(a['evidence count'] - item['evidence count']), abs(a['call_id'] - item['call_id'])))\n    89\t        matched.append(dict(best, matches=f'{item[\"source\"]}:{item[\"call_id\"]}'))\n    90\t    sealed = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{t2_offline.SEALED}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    91\t    sealed_largest = None\n    92\t    for call_id, request_json, tokens_in, tokens_out in sealed.execute(\"select call_id, request_json, input_tokens, output_tokens from model_calls where purpose='judge'\"):\n    93\t        item = entry('sealed-D358', call_id, json.loads(request_json), tokens_in, tokens_out)\n    94\t        if sealed_largest is None or item['evidence count'] > sealed_largest['evidence count']:\n    95\t            sealed_largest = item\n    96\t    plan = {'acceptance': [largest, sealed_largest], 'refused': refused, 'matched': matched}\n    97\t    sends = {'acceptance': 1, 'refused': 3, 'matched under v12': 3, 'matched under v7 (control)': 3}\n    98\t    estimate = (sum(a['estimated cost'] for a in plan['acceptance']) + 3 * sum(r['estimated cost'] for r in refused)\n    99\t                + 6 * sum(m['estimated cost'] for m in matched))\n   100\t    plan['sends'], plan['estimate'] = sends, round(estimate, 4)\n   101\t    (HERE / 'probe-plan.json').write_text(json.dumps(plan, ensure_ascii=False))\n   102\t    print(json.dumps({'acceptance': [(a['source'], a['call_id'], a['evidence count'], a['estimated cost']) for a in plan['acceptance']],\n   103\t                      'refused': [(r['source'], r['call_id'], r['view'], r['evidence count'], r['bad ids']) for r in refused],\n   104\t                      'matched': [(m['source'], m['call_id'], m['view'], m['evidence count'], m['matches']) for m in matched],\n   105\t                      'sends': sends, 'estimate (stored tokens, published rates)': plan['estimate']}, indent=1))\n   106\t\n   107\t\n   108\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   109\t    main()\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff  2067749 Nov 22 00:28 docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe-plan.json\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'command -v claude; claude --help'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'command -v claude; claude --help'","aggregated_output":"/opt/homebrew/bin/claude\nUsage: claude [options] [command] [prompt]\n\nClaude Code - starts an interactive session by default, use -p/--print for\nnon-interactive output\n\nArguments:\n  prompt                                Your prompt\n\nOptions:\n  --add-dir <directories...>            Additional directories to allow tool\n                                        access to\n  --agent <agent>                       Agent for the current session. Overrides\n                                        the 'agent' setting.\n  --agents <json-or-file>               JSON object defining custom agents, or\n                                        with --print the path to a file that\n                                        holds one (e.g. '{\"reviewer\":\n                                        {\"description\": \"Reviews code\",\n                                        \"prompt\": \"You are a code reviewer\"}}')\n  --allow-dangerously-skip-permissions  Enable bypassing all permission checks\n                                        as an option, without it being enabled\n                                        by default. Recommended only for\n                                        sandboxes with no internet access.\n  --allowedTools, --allowed-tools <tools...>\n      Comma or space-separated list of tool names to allow (e.g. \"Bash(git *)\n      Edit\")\n  --append-system-prompt <prompt>       Append a system prompt to the default\n                                        system prompt\n  --autocompact <auto|tokens>           Auto-compact window size (auto, or\n                                        100k–1M tokens)\n  --ax-screen-reader                    Render screen-reader friendly output\n                                        (flat text, no decorative borders or\n                                        animations).\n  --bg, --background                    Start the session in the background and\n                                        return immediately. Prints the id that\n                                        `claude attach`, `logs`, `stop` and `rm`\n                                        take; `claude agents` lists them. With\n                                        --resume <session-id>, continues that\n                                        session in the background under the same\n                                        ID, or starts a copy and says so when\n                                        the session is already running\n  --bare                                Minimal mode: skip hooks (those defined\n                                        in settings and by installed plugins;\n                                        features built into Claude Code are\n                                        unaffected), LSP, plugin sync,\n                                        attribution, auto-memory, background\n                                        prefetches, keychain reads, and\n                                        CLAUDE.md auto-discovery. Sets\n                                        CLAUDE_CODE_SIMPLE=1. Anthropic auth is\n                                        strictly ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or\n                                        apiKeyHelper via --settings (OAuth and\n                                        keychain are never read). 3P providers\n                                        (Bedrock/Vertex/Foundry) use their own\n                                        credentials. Skills still resolve via\n                                        /skill-name. Explicitly provide context\n                                        via: --system-prompt[-file],\n                                        --append-system-prompt[-file], --add-dir\n                                        (CLAUDE.md dirs), --mcp-config,\n                                        --settings, --agents, --plugin-dir.\n  --betas <betas...>                    Beta headers to include in API requests\n                                        (API key users only)\n  --brief                               Enable SendUserMessage tool for\n                                        agent-to-user communication\n  --chrome                              Enable Claude in Chrome integration\n  --cloud [description|session_id|url]  Create a cloud session with the given\n                                        description, or attach to an existing\n                                        one by session ID or claude.ai/code URL\n  -c, --continue                        Continue the most recent conversation in\n                                        the current directory\n  --dangerously-skip-permissions        Bypass all permission checks.\n                                        Recommended only for sandboxes with no\n                                        internet access.\n  -d, --debug [filter]                  Enable debug mode with optional category\n                                        filtering (e.g., --debug=hooks,mcp or\n                                        --debug='!hooks,!mcp')\n  --debug-file <path>                   Write debug logs to a specific file path\n                                        (implicitly enables debug mode)\n  --desktop                             Open in the Claude Desktop app instead\n                                        of the terminal (with --continue or\n                                        --resume <id> to pick the session)\n  --disable-slash-commands              Disable all skills and commands\n  --disallowedTools, --disallowed-tools <tools...>\n      Comma or space-separated list of tool names to deny (e.g. \"Bash(git *)\n      Edit\")\n  --effort <level>                      Effort level for the current session\n                                        (low, medium, high, xhigh, max)\n  --environment <environment_id>        Create a new cloud session that runs on\n                                        the given self-hosted environment\n                                        (ccpool_...).\n  --exclude-dynamic-system-prompt-sections\n      Move per-machine sections (cwd, env info, memory paths, git status) from\n      the system prompt into the first user message. Improves cross-user\n      prompt-cache reuse. Only applies with the default system prompt (ignored\n      with --system-prompt). (default: false)\n  --fallback-model <model>              Enable automatic fallback to specified\n                                        model(s) when the default model is\n                                        overloaded or not available. Accepts a\n                                        comma-separated list to try each in\n                                        order. Re-tries the primary at the start\n                                        of each user turn.\n  --file <specs...>                     File resources to download at startup.\n                                        Format: file_id:relative_path (e.g.,\n                                        --file file_abc:doc.txt\n                                        file_def:img.png)\n  --fork-session                        When resuming, create a new session ID\n                                        instead of reusing the original (use\n                                        with --resume or --continue)\n  --forward-subagent-text               Forward subagent text and thinking\n                                        blocks as assistant/user messages with\n                                        parent_tool_use_id set (only works with\n                                        --print and --output-format=stream-json)\n  --from-pr [value]                     Resume a session linked to a PR by PR\n                                        number/URL, or open interactive picker\n                                        with optional search term\n  -h, --help                            Display help for command\n  --ide                                 Automatically connect to IDE on startup\n                                        if exactly one valid IDE is available\n  --include-hook-events                 Include all hook lifecycle events in the\n                                        output stream (only works with\n                                        --output-format=stream-json)\n  --include-partial-messages            Include partial message chunks as they\n                                        arrive (only works with --print and\n                                        --output-format=stream-json)\n  --input-format <format>               Input format (only works with --print):\n                                        \"text\" (default), or \"stream-json\"\n                                        (realtime streaming input) (choices:\n                                        \"text\", \"stream-json\")\n  --json-schema <schema>                JSON Schema for structured output\n                                        validation (only works with --print).\n                                        Example:\n                                        {\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"name\":{\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"name\"]}\n  --max-budget-usd <amount>             Maximum dollar amount to spend on API\n                                        calls (only works with --print)\n  --mcp-config <configs...>             Load MCP servers from JSON files or\n                                        strings (space-separated)\n  --model <model>                       Model for the current session. Provide\n                                        an alias for the latest model (e.g.\n                                        'fable', 'opus', or 'sonnet') or a\n                                        model's full name.\n  -n, --name <name>                     Set a display name for this session\n                                        (shown in the prompt box, /resume\n                                        picker, and terminal title)\n  --no-chrome                           Disable Claude in Chrome integration\n  --no-session-persistence              Disable session persistence - sessions\n                                        will not be saved to disk and cannot be\n                                        resumed (only works with --print)\n  --output-format <format>              Output format (only works with --print):\n                                        \"text\" (default), \"json\" (single\n                                        result), or \"stream-json\" (realtime\n                                        streaming) (choices: \"text\", \"json\",\n                                        \"stream-json\")\n  --permission-mode <mode>              Permission mode to use for the session\n                                        (choices: \"acceptEdits\", \"auto\",\n                                        \"bypassPermissions\", \"manual\",\n                                        \"dontAsk\", \"plan\")\n  --permission-prompts <target>         Who answers permission prompts with\n                                        --print: \"host\" (the SDK host or\n                                        --permission-prompt-tool) or \"none\"\n                                        (nobody: anything that would prompt is\n                                        denied automatically; the permission\n                                        mode still decides everything else)\n                                        (choices: \"host\", \"none\", default:\n                                        \"host\")\n  --plugin-dir <path>                   Load a plugin from a directory or .zip\n                                        for this session only; a folder of\n                                        plugins loads each child (repeatable:\n                                        --plugin-dir A --plugin-dir B.zip)\n                                        (default: [])\n  --plugin-url <url>                    Fetch a plugin .zip from a URL for this\n                                        session only (repeatable: --plugin-url A\n                                        --plugin-url B) (default: [])\n  -p, --print                           Print response and exit (useful for\n                                        pipes). Note: The workspace trust dialog\n                                        is skipped when Claude is run in\n                                        non-interactive mode (via -p, or when\n                                        stdout is not a TTY, e.g. piped or\n                                        redirected output). Only use this in\n                                        directories you trust. Settings files\n                                        that fail validation are silently\n                                        ignored in this mode (no error dialog is\n                                        shown).\n  --prompt-suggestions [value]          Enable prompt suggestions. In print/SDK\n                                        mode, emits a prompt_suggestion message\n                                        after each turn with a predicted next\n                                        user prompt (choices: \"true\", \"false\",\n                                        \"1\", \"0\", \"yes\", \"no\", \"on\", \"off\",\n                                        preset: \"true\")\n  --remote-control [name]               Start an interactive session with Remote\n                                        Control enabled (optionally named)\n  --remote-control-session-name-prefix <prefix>\n      Prefix for auto-generated Remote Control session names (default: hostname)\n  --replay-user-messages                Re-emit user messages from stdin back on\n                                        stdout for acknowledgment (only works\n                                        with --input-format=stream-json and\n                                        --output-format=stream-json)\n  --restricted                          Restricted mode: removes the built-in\n                                        tools that run commands or code (Bash,\n                                        PowerShell and the other code-running\n                                        tools) and WebFetch unless --tools names\n                                        them, and ignores user, project and\n                                        local settings files (managed settings\n                                        and --settings still apply; add\n                                        --strict-mcp-config to skip MCP servers\n                                        too). Also confines the file tools to\n                                        the working directories (--add-dir\n                                        included), refuses bypassPermissions,\n                                        and lets only a person or the configured\n                                        permission handler approve writes to\n                                        settings, git and tool-configuration\n                                        files.\n  -r, --resume [value]                  Resume a conversation by session ID or\n                                        name, or open interactive picker with\n                                        optional search term\n  --safe-mode                           Start with all customizations\n                                        (CLAUDE.md, skills, installed plugins,\n                                        hooks, MCP servers, custom commands and\n                                        agents, output styles, workflows, custom\n                                        themes, keybindings, and more) disabled\n                                        — useful for troubleshooting a broken\n                                        configuration. Admin-managed (policy)\n                                        settings still apply. Auth, model\n                                        selection, built-in tools and plugins,\n                                        and permissions work normally. Sets\n                                        CLAUDE_CODE_SAFE_MODE=1.\n  --session-id <uuid>                   Use a specific session ID for the\n                                        conversation (must be a valid UUID)\n  --setting-sources <sources>           Comma-separated list of setting sources\n                                        to load (user, project, local).\n  --settings <file-or-json>             Path to a settings JSON file or a JSON\n                                        string to load additional settings from\n  --strict-mcp-config                   Only use MCP servers from --mcp-config,\n                                        ignoring all other MCP configurations\n  --system-prompt <prompt>              System prompt to use for the session\n  --system-prompt-snapshot <on|off>     Record the system prompt once per\n                                        conversation and reuse it verbatim on\n                                        every request and resume. on (the\n                                        default, except under --bare outside\n                                        cloud and background sessions): the\n                                        prompt is rendered on the conversation's\n                                        first request — a --system-prompt or\n                                        --append-system-prompt included — sent,\n                                        and recorded; every later request and\n                                        resume sends the record as-is, even when\n                                        a later launch passes different text,\n                                        until the conversation is compacted.\n                                        off: never record; the prompt is\n                                        rendered fresh every request (for\n                                        iterating on prompt text). (choices:\n                                        \"on\", \"off\")\n  --teleport [session]                  Resume a teleport session, optionally\n                                        specify session ID\n  --tmux                                Create a tmux session for the worktree\n                                        (requires --worktree). Uses iTerm2\n                                        native panes when available; use\n                                        --tmux=classic for traditional tmux.\n  --tools <tools...>                    Specify the list of available tools from\n                                        the built-in set. Use \"\" to disable all\n                                        tools, \"default\" to use the default set,\n                                        or specify tool names (e.g.\n                                        \"Bash,Edit,Read\").\n  --verbose                             Override verbose mode setting from\n                                        config\n  -v, --version                         Output the version number\n  -w, --worktree [name]                 Create a new git worktree for this\n                                        session (optionally specify a name)\n\nCommands:\n  agents [options]                      Manage background agents\n  attach <id|name>                      Open a background session in this\n                                        terminal. <id> is the short id that\n                                        `claude --bg` prints and `claude agents`\n                                        lists. Part of the session name works\n                                        too\n  auth                                  Manage authentication\n  auto-mode                             Inspect or reset auto mode configuration\n  doctor                                Check the health of your Claude Code\n                                        installation. Reads settings files in\n                                        the current directory without a trust\n                                        prompt. For a full checkup that can also\n                                        fix issues, run /doctor in a session.\n  gateway [options]                     Run the enterprise auth/telemetry\n                                        gateway\n  import [options] [source]             Import config from another AI coding\n                                        agent into Claude Code\n  install [options] [target]            Install Claude Code native build. Use\n                                        [target] to specify version (stable,\n                                        latest, or specific version)\n  logs <id|name>                        Print a background session's recent\n                                        terminal output\n  mcp                                   Configure and manage MCP servers\n  plugin|plugins                        Manage Claude Code plugins\n  purge [options] [path]                Delete a project's Claude Code data\n                                        (transcripts, tasks, file history, and\n                                        its entry in ~/.claude.json) from this\n                                        computer only\n  respawn [options] [id]                Restart a background session, or all of\n                                        them with --all, so it runs the current\n                                        Claude Code version\n  rm <id>                               Delete a background session, and its\n                                        worktree when that is safe. Works on\n                                        sessions that have already exited\n  setup-token                           Set up a long-lived authentication token\n                                        (requires Claude subscription)\n  stop|kill <id>                        Stop a background session. Its\n                                        conversation is kept: `claude attach\n                                        <id>` opens it again, `claude --resume`\n                                        works once it is stopped\n  ultrareview [options] [target]        Run a cloud-hosted multi-agent code\n                                        review of the current branch (or a PR\n                                        number / base branch) and print the\n                                        findings\n  update|upgrade                        Check for updates and install if\n                                        available; package-manager installs\n                                        update through the package manager\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,201p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; tail -n 5 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba src/hivemind/model.py; nl -ba clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/budget_replay.py'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,201p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; tail -n 5 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n\n> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n\n## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n\nBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n\n1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n   word for it is already given.\n4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n\n## 1. Goal (end to end)\n\nEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\nnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\nany one step's own score.\n\n## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n\n**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n\nThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n\n- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n  nothing at all and were never judged.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n| Evidence | Content | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n\nAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n\n## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n\n| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n\n## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n\n- **The frozen ruler** (AGENTS §7.1, D261, D262): the extraction standard, version 5 (the signed version 3 with D248's seven boundaries and D250's four\n  evidence boundaries and counting unit), golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold; not a word changes; a disagreement is a gap in the\n  system (D263).\n- **The frozen extraction prompt** (D274): v74i (`poc-v108`) does not change by a word; what this phase changes is the one place that holds the filing\n  rule (the description of the `links` field), made into a new version with a new configuration and compared with v74i. Alex has given his word for this\n  work (2026-11-04: \"好你去解决问题\", \"开始做\"); making it the default needs his word again.\n- **Frozen opening and judging** (D354): this phase does not touch them.\n- **The standard's text** (in translation): §6, \"file under the narrowest open question; when no question takes it, record no home and drop it, and read\n  each one at evaluation\"; §5.1, \"makes the answer to some question more or less likely\", with four paths, \"demand, supply, competition, pricing\"; §5.3,\n  \"another company's matter is not kept when one cannot say which question about the covered company it moves through a path such as demand, supply,\n  competition or pricing\".\n- **Decisions**: D207 (one home per fact, the narrowest question), D209 (no home means dropped, each one read), D259 (leeway: a figure question takes\n  \"the demand, supply, competition and pricing that make it more or less likely\"; the total-revenue question taking more is an accepted cost, \"bounded by\n  the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\"), D265 (code keeps only the narrowest), D273 (the fallback sentence), D355 (the two failed tests\n  of the previous filing topic).\n- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n\n## 5. What to do\n\nStatus: the links-rule version that carried items 1–3 (v74l) failed its one test (§8 step 5); D361 keeps the fallback sentence, so item 3 is not carried out. The plan below is kept as written.\n\n1. **Replace the abstract sentence \"makes the answer more or less likely through demand, supply, competition or pricing\" with the few classes in which an analyst recognises an indirect relation, written as a closed set, each class with two or three examples from other industries** (AGENTS §6: one\n   abstract sentence is a coin flip on every send; examples replace it). A first draft of the classes (checked against the standard line by line at\n   design time; it holds only meanings the standard already has):\n   - The question's own figure: this question's figures, guidance, estimates and revisions (estimates for another period or another population follow\n     the existing rule).\n   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n   maker's share question); when one sentence gives a separate figure for each of two questions, write two items, each filed under its own question\n   (code stores two items that quote the same sentence as one record linked to both questions); never also file it under a broader question about the\n   same company (the failure of D355's test 2: the model filed it under an NVIDIA revenue question as well). The original draft's \"when the other\n   party's question is open … file it there as well\" conflicted with D207 and was deleted on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement (D360).\n3. **Delete the fallback sentence** and return to the standard's §6: when no question takes a fact, it is not filed; code drops it as \"no home\" and\n   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n\n## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n\n- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n  measured and reported apart.\n- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n  counted after.\n- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n    question\";\n  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n    or no question uses);\n  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n    ones that matter one by one.\n- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n      facts must not fall.\n  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n      what was measured.\n  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n\n## 7. Process (no drift)\n\n- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n  sentence, in both directions.\n- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n\n## 8. Steps and where we are\n\n| # | Step | Output | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n\n## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n\n- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n| 37 | 11-05 | Alex：\"为什么没过？…你确定是正确的rejection 吗？你又下去看trace吗？\"；\"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信下一步你要跑什么才能收敛\"。普查全部 7 次读法、606 次文件读（`census_all_reads.py`，$0） | 第三版 0 个错数、156 次读漏 3 次（全是资本开支）；第二版 2 个错数；第四版漏 24 次（5 月）、16 次（8 月）。我自己判错的：6 个对的数被我的引用检查拒（漏星号、跳格），8 月\"只认本季\"规则错，第 13 轮 3 条\"有争议\"的数是标准答案自己用的行，第三版全过后又改第四版 | 收敛方案：提示词定为第三版不再改；修引用检查（$0 重放全部 606 次读核对）；在 8 月财报上用第三版读一次（约 8 美分，只有\"收错数\"能叫停）；然后做进生产 |\n| 38 | 11-05 | Alex：\"好，按这三步做，你不要再搬石头砸自己脚了，你要收敛上生产\"。第 1 步：引用检查搬进正式代码 `src/hivemind/baseline.py`，加\"行名是报告的、数在同一行就存原文那一整行\"的修复（漏脚注记号、跳格）；对照 36 例全对，三个弄坏的版本各抓出 1 个错；重放全部存下的读法，改变的正好是那 6 个被拒的对数。第 2 步：第三版加修好的检查读 8 月留出财报 132 次（0.096 美元），两人分类 | 第 1 步：只改了那 6 条。第 2 步过：44 个数 0 个错（3 条\"剩余资本开支承诺 70 亿\"两人不一致，对着原文定为\"自己的量，别的期间\"）；v10 3/3、v12 2/3 | 读法定为第三版加修好的检查，不再改；第 3 步写正式代码 |\n| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba src/hivemind/model.py; nl -ba clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/budget_replay.py'","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"One model call, archived and replayable.\n     2\t\n     3\t`call()` sends a system and user message with a strict JSON schema to an OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint (OpenAI,\n     4\tor DeepSeek through the same wire format), checks the answer against the schema, and records the exact request and\n     5\traw answer in an archive (SQLite, a POC artefact kept beside the test database, never inside the views database).\n     6\tA request whose sha256 is already in the archive is answered from it without a network call, so a rerun of a\n     7\treplay costs nothing and gives the same answers. The provider key is read from the owner-only credentials file and\n     8\tnever appears in the archive, logs or arguments.\n     9\t\"\"\"\n    10\timport dataclasses\n    11\timport base64\n    12\timport hashlib\n    13\timport http.client\n    14\timport json\n    15\timport re\n    16\timport os\n    17\timport sqlite3\n    18\timport time\n    19\timport uuid\n    20\timport urllib.error\n    21\timport urllib.request\n    22\t\n    23\tCREDENTIALS = os.path.expanduser('~/.config/hivemind/credentials.json')\n    24\tENDPOINTS = {'openai': ('https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions', 'OPENAI_API_KEY'),\n    25\t             'deepseek': ('https://api.deepseek.com/chat/completions', 'DEEPSEEK_API_KEY')}\n    26\t# USD per million tokens, for the cost column only (an estimate; the provider's bill is the measurement)\n    27\t# gpt-6.1-sol: the published standard rates on 2026-11-16 (developers.openai.com/api/docs/models: \"$2 / $10\" per million input and output\n    28\t# tokens; D628's model comparison only). gpt-6-astra: the published standard rates on 2026-11-16 (developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/\n    29\t# gpt-6-astra: \"$10 input … $50 output\" per million tokens; D632's comparison only).\n    30\t# claude-opus-5-5: Anthropic's first-party list price, $4 input and $20 output per million tokens (the claude-api skill's model table,\n    31\t# cached 2026-11-03); Alex's Opus trial only (2026-11-16, \"你试试看opus\"). Cache reads ($0.20) are priced at CACHED_INPUT_SHARE, an over-estimate.\n    32\t# gpt-6-luna: the published standard rates on 2026-10-25 (developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-6-luna: $0.10/M input, $0.50/M output,\n    33\t# cached input $0.01/M). Until this date the table carried (1.25, 10.0), so every cost_usd recorded before it is 12.5x (input) to 20x (output)\n    34\t# the published rate; the token columns are the measurement, the bill is the provider's.\n    35\tPRICES = {'gpt-6-luna': (0.10, 0.50), 'gpt-6.1-sol': (2.00, 10.00), 'gpt-6-astra': (10.00, 50.00), 'claude-opus-5-5': (4.00, 20.00), 'deepseek-flash': (0.30, 1.20), 'deepseek-v4-pro': (1.32, 3.96),\n    36\t          'jev-1.14.0': (0.042, 0.0)}   # Jev: docs.typesafe.ai/models, 2026-10-25; output free   # DeepSeek: peak-hour list prices, cache misses (2026-10-23 pricing page)\n    37\tCACHED_INPUT_SHARE = 0.10          # cached input tokens are priced at 10% of the input rate (gpt-6-luna page, read again 2026-10-27)\n    38\t\n    39\tSCHEMA_SQL = \"\"\"\n    40\tcreate table if not exists model_calls (\n    41\t  call_id integer primary key autoincrement, purpose text, config_version text, config_checksum text, model text,\n    42\t  request_sha256 text unique, request_json text, response_json text, answer_json text, prompt_chars integer,\n    43\t  input_tokens integer, output_tokens integer, cost_usd real, seconds real, created_at text\n    44\t);\n    45\tcreate index if not exists model_calls_sha on model_calls(request_sha256);\n    46\t\"\"\"\n    47\t\n    48\t\n    49\tclass SchemaError(ValueError):\n    50\t    pass\n    51\t\n    52\t\n    53\tclass TransportError(RuntimeError):\n    54\t    pass\n    55\t\n    56\t\n    57\t@dataclasses.dataclass\n    58\tclass Answer:\n    59\t    data: dict\n    60\t    call_id: int\n    61\t    request_sha256: str\n    62\t    cached: bool\n    63\t    prompt_chars: int\n    64\t    input_tokens: int\n    65\t    output_tokens: int\n    66\t    cost_usd: float\n    67\t\n    68\t\n    69\tclass Archive:\n    70\t    \"\"\"Shared by the extraction worker threads: one connection, every access under a lock.\"\"\"\n    71\t\n    72\t    def __init__(self, path):\n    73\t        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path)), exist_ok=True)\n    74\t        self.conn = sqlite3.connect(path, check_same_thread=False)\n    75\t        self.conn.executescript(SCHEMA_SQL)\n    76\t        import threading\n    77\t        self.lock = threading.Lock()\n    78\t\n    79\t    def lookup(self, sha):\n    80\t        with self.lock:\n    81\t            return self.conn.execute('select call_id, answer_json, prompt_chars, input_tokens, output_tokens from model_calls where request_sha256=?', (sha,)).fetchone()\n    82\t\n    83\t    def record(self, **fields):\n    84\t        \"\"\"One row per distinct request; a second identical request recorded at the same moment (two threads, or a\n    85\t        failed answer repeated within the same second) keeps the first row's id instead of failing.\"\"\"\n    86\t        columns = list(fields)\n    87\t        with self.lock:\n    88\t            cursor = self.conn.execute(f'insert or ignore into model_calls ({\",\".join(columns)}) values ({\",\".join(\"?\" * len(columns))})',\n    89\t                                       [fields[c] for c in columns])\n    90\t            self.conn.commit()\n    91\t            if cursor.rowcount == 0:\n    92\t                return self.conn.execute('select call_id from model_calls where request_sha256=?', (fields['request_sha256'],)).fetchone()[0]\n    93\t            return cursor.lastrowid\n    94\t\n    95\t    def retire(self, call_id):\n    96\t        \"\"\"A code check refused this answer: the row stays for the record, but no later identical request is answered with it\n    97\t        (the same shape as a schema-refused or malformed row, whose hash carries a suffix).\"\"\"\n    98\t        with self.lock:\n    99\t            self.conn.execute(\"update model_calls set request_sha256 = request_sha256 || ':refused:' || ? where call_id=? and request_sha256 not like '%:refused:%'\",\n   100\t                              (uuid.uuid4().hex[:8], call_id))\n   101\t            self.conn.commit()\n   102\t\n   103\t    def totals(self):\n   104\t        with self.lock:\n   105\t            return self.conn.execute('select count(*), coalesce(sum(cost_usd),0), coalesce(sum(input_tokens),0), coalesce(sum(output_tokens),0) from model_calls').fetchone()\n   106\t\n   107\t\n   108\tdef _key(provider):\n   109\t    name = ENDPOINTS[provider][1]\n   110\t    if os.environ.get(name):\n   111\t        return os.environ[name]\n   112\t    with open(CREDENTIALS, encoding='utf-8') as handle:\n   113\t        return json.load(handle)[name]\n   114\t\n   115\t\n   116\tdef build_request(setting, system, user, schema, schema_name, provider):\n   117\t    request = {\n   118\t        'model': setting.model,\n   119\t        'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': system}, {'role': 'user', 'content': user}],\n   120\t        'response_format': {'type': 'json_schema', 'json_schema': {'name': schema_name, 'strict': True, 'schema': schema}},\n   121\t    }\n   122\t    if provider == 'openai':\n   123\t        request['max_completion_tokens'] = setting.max_output_tokens\n   124\t        if setting.reasoning_effort:\n   125\t            request['reasoning_effort'] = setting.reasoning_effort\n   126\t    else:\n   127\t        request['max_tokens'] = setting.max_output_tokens\n   128\t    return request\n   129\t\n   130\t\n   131\tdef openai_transport(provider, request, timeout=180, max_attempts=3):   # the default preserves the production retry behavior\n   132\t    url, _ = ENDPOINTS[provider]\n   133\t    body = json.dumps(request).encode('utf-8')\n   134\t    headers = {'Authorization': f'Bearer {_key(provider)}', 'Content-Type': 'application/json'}\n   135\t    last = None\n   136\t    last_http = None\n   137\t    if isinstance(max_attempts, bool) or not isinstance(max_attempts, int) or max_attempts < 1:\n   138\t        raise ValueError('max_attempts must be a positive integer')\n   139\t    for attempt in range(max_attempts):\n   140\t        try:\n   141\t            with urllib.request.urlopen(urllib.request.Request(url, data=body, headers=headers, method='POST'), timeout=timeout) as response:\n   142\t                raw_bytes = response.read()\n   143\t                try:\n   144\t                    raw = raw_bytes.decode('utf-8')\n   145\t                except UnicodeDecodeError:\n   146\t                    if max_attempts != 1:\n   147\t                        raise\n   148\t                    failure = TransportError('invalid UTF-8 HTTP response')\n   149\t                    failure.response_body = raw_bytes.decode('utf-8', 'replace')\n   150\t                    failure.response_body_base64 = base64.b64encode(raw_bytes).decode('ascii')\n   151\t                    failure.http_status = response.status\n   152\t                    raise failure\n   153\t                try:\n   154\t                    return json.loads(raw)\n   155\t                except json.JSONDecodeError:\n   156\t                    if max_attempts != 1:\n   157\t                        raise\n   158\t                    failure = TransportError('malformed HTTP response')\n   159\t                    failure.response_body = raw\n   160\t                    failure.http_status = response.status\n   161\t                    raise failure\n   162\t        except urllib.error.HTTPError as error:\n   163\t            body_bytes = error.read()\n   164\t            body_text = body_bytes.decode('utf-8', 'replace' if max_attempts == 1 else 'ignore')\n   165\t            last_http = (error.code, body_text, base64.b64encode(body_bytes).decode('ascii'))\n   166\t            last = f'HTTP {error.code}: {body_text[:600]}'\n   167\t            if error.code in (400, 401, 403, 404):\n   168\t                failure = TransportError(last)\n   169\t                failure.response_body = body_text\n   170\t                failure.response_body_base64 = last_http[2]\n   171\t                failure.http_status = error.code\n   172\t                raise failure\n   173\t        except (urllib.error.URLError, TimeoutError, ConnectionError, OSError, http.client.HTTPException) as error:   # a dropped or truncated response is retried like a network error\n   174\t            last = repr(error)[:200]\n   175\t            last_http = None   # the final failure describes the last attempt only\n   176\t        if max_attempts == 3 or attempt + 1 < max_attempts:\n   177\t            time.sleep(5 * (attempt + 1))\n   178\t    failure = TransportError(last or 'no answer')\n   179\t    if last_http:\n   180\t        failure.http_status, failure.response_body, failure.response_body_base64 = last_http\n   181\t    raise failure\n   182\t\n   183\t\n   184\tdef check_schema(value, schema, path='$', root=None):\n   185\t    \"\"\"A small validator for the subset of JSON Schema the prompts use: object/required/additionalProperties,\n   186\t    array/items/minItems/maxItems, string/enum/pattern, integer, number, boolean, null in anyOf, and $ref into the root's $defs.\"\"\"\n   187\t    root = root if root is not None else schema\n   188\t    if '$ref' in schema:\n   189\t        name = schema['$ref'].rsplit('/', 1)[1]\n   190\t        schema = root['$defs'][name]\n   191\t    kind = schema.get('type')\n   192\t    if 'anyOf' in schema:\n   193\t        # a value matching no option reports the closest options' errors (fewest), not merely the last option's\n   194\t        failures = []\n   195\t        for option in schema['anyOf']:\n   196\t            errors = check_schema(value, option, path, root)\n   197\t            if not errors:\n   198\t                return []\n   199\t            failures.append(errors)\n   200\t        fewest = min((len(errors) for errors in failures), default=0)\n   201\t        closest = list(dict.fromkeys(error for errors in failures if len(errors) == fewest for error in errors))\n   202\t        return closest or [f'{path}: matches no option']\n   203\t    if kind == 'object':\n   204\t        if not isinstance(value, dict):\n   205\t            return [f'{path}: expected object']\n   206\t        errors = []\n   207\t        for name in schema.get('required', []):\n   208\t            if name not in value:\n   209\t                errors.append(f'{path}.{name}: required')\n   210\t        properties = schema.get('properties', {})\n   211\t        for name, item in value.items():\n   212\t            if name in properties:\n   213\t                errors.extend(check_schema(item, properties[name], f'{path}.{name}', root))\n   214\t            elif schema.get('additionalProperties') is False:\n   215\t                errors.append(f'{path}.{name}: not allowed')\n   216\t        return errors\n   217\t    if kind == 'array':\n   218\t        if not isinstance(value, list):\n   219\t            return [f'{path}: expected array']\n   220\t        errors = []\n   221\t        if len(value) < schema.get('minItems', 0):\n   222\t            errors.append(f'{path}: fewer than {schema[\"minItems\"]} items')\n   223\t        if 'maxItems' in schema and len(value) > schema['maxItems']:\n   224\t            errors.append(f'{path}: more than {schema[\"maxItems\"]} items')\n   225\t        for index, item in enumerate(value):\n   226\t            errors.extend(check_schema(item, schema.get('items', {}), f'{path}[{index}]', root))\n   227\t        return errors\n   228\t    if kind == 'string':\n   229\t        if not isinstance(value, str):\n   230\t            return [f'{path}: expected string']\n   231\t        if 'enum' in schema and value not in schema['enum']:\n   232\t            return [f'{path}: {value!r} not in {schema[\"enum\"]}']\n   233\t        if 'pattern' in schema and not re.search(schema['pattern'], value):\n   234\t            return [f'{path}: {value!r} does not match {schema[\"pattern\"]}']\n   235\t        return []\n   236\t    if kind == 'integer':\n   237\t        return [] if isinstance(value, int) and not isinstance(value, bool) else [f'{path}: expected integer']\n   238\t    if kind == 'number':\n   239\t        return [] if isinstance(value, (int, float)) and not isinstance(value, bool) else [f'{path}: expected number']\n   240\t    if kind == 'boolean':\n   241\t        return [] if isinstance(value, bool) else [f'{path}: expected boolean']\n   242\t    if kind == 'null':\n   243\t        return [] if value is None else [f'{path}: expected null']\n   244\t    return []\n   245\t\n   246\t\n   247\tdef request_identity(request):\n   248\t    \"\"\"The exact serialized request and its archive key, shared by callers and validation.\"\"\"\n   249\t    encoded = json.dumps(request, sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False)\n   250\t    return encoded, hashlib.sha256(encoded.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest()\n   251\t\n   252\t\n   253\tdef call(archive, cfg, setting, system, user, schema, schema_name, purpose, *, transport=None):\n   254\t    provider = cfg.provider\n   255\t    request = build_request(setting, system, user, schema, schema_name, provider)\n   256\t    request_json, sha = request_identity(request)\n   257\t    hit = archive.lookup(sha)\n   258\t    prompt_chars = len(system) + len(user)\n   259\t    if hit:\n   260\t        call_id, answer_json, _, input_tokens, output_tokens = hit\n   261\t        return Answer(json.loads(answer_json), call_id, sha, True, prompt_chars, input_tokens or 0, output_tokens or 0, 0.0)\n   262\t    started = time.time()\n   263\t    response = (transport or openai_transport)(provider, request)\n   264\t    seconds = round(time.time() - started, 1)\n   265\t    try:\n   266\t        content = response['choices'][0]['message']['content']\n   267\t        data = json.loads(content)\n   268\t    except (KeyError, IndexError, TypeError, json.JSONDecodeError) as error:\n   269\t        archive.record(purpose=purpose, config_version=cfg.version, config_checksum=cfg.checksum, model=setting.model,\n   270\t                       request_sha256=sha + ':malformed:' + uuid.uuid4().hex[:8], request_json=request_json,\n   271\t                       response_json=json.dumps(response)[:200000], answer_json=None, prompt_chars=prompt_chars,\n   272\t                       input_tokens=0, output_tokens=0, cost_usd=0.0, seconds=seconds, created_at=time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S'))\n   273\t        raise SchemaError(f'malformed answer: {error}')\n   274\t    errors = check_schema(data, schema)\n   275\t    usage = response.get('usage') or {}\n   276\t    input_tokens = int(usage.get('prompt_tokens') or 0)\n   277\t    output_tokens = int(usage.get('completion_tokens') or 0)\n   278\t    cached_tokens = min(input_tokens, int((usage.get('prompt_tokens_details') or {}).get('cached_tokens') or 0))\n   279\t    price_in, price_out = PRICES.get(setting.model, (0.0, 0.0))\n   280\t    cost = round((input_tokens - cached_tokens) / 1e6 * price_in + cached_tokens / 1e6 * price_in * CACHED_INPUT_SHARE + output_tokens / 1e6 * price_out, 6)\n   281\t    call_id = archive.record(purpose=purpose, config_version=cfg.version, config_checksum=cfg.checksum, model=setting.model,\n   282\t                             request_sha256=sha if not errors else sha + ':schema:' + uuid.uuid4().hex[:8], request_json=request_json,\n   283\t                             response_json=json.dumps(response)[:200000], answer_json=json.dumps(data, ensure_ascii=False),\n   284\t                             prompt_chars=prompt_chars, input_tokens=input_tokens, output_tokens=output_tokens, cost_usd=cost,\n   285\t                             seconds=seconds, created_at=time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S'))\n   286\t    if errors:\n   287\t        raise SchemaError('; '.join(errors[:5]))\n   288\t    return Answer(data, call_id, sha, False, prompt_chars, input_tokens, output_tokens, cost)\n     1\t\"\"\"Production as it now runs (`scripts/replay.py`, `poc-v118`), with every paid call held to one shared ceiling (D442's comparison,\n     2\tdesign shape B'). The replay is `hivemind.replay.run` with the same arguments `scripts/replay.py` passes; the only difference is the\n     3\ttransport, which the production code already accepts (`replay.run(transport=...)`, passed to extraction, matching, the collision judge\n     4\tand the day's judging): `BudgetTransport` sends each request exactly as the production transports do, after reserving its worst cost\n     5\ton a ledger shared by every run under one ceiling.\n     6\t\n     7\tReservation per attempt: a language-model request reserves (2 × its JSON bytes + 4,096) input tokens and its whole output cap\n     8\t(`max_completion_tokens`) at `model.PRICES`; a Jev request reserves (2 × its bytes + 4,096) input tokens at Jev's input rate, times\n     9\tJev's own three attempts (`jev.transport` retries inside one call). A Jev call settles at three times its own reported input tokens\n    10\t(a hidden retry is the same request, billed at most the same input; Jev's output is free), the whole charge settled so that one above\n    11\tthe reservation sets the overrun flag; usage missing, invalid or zero settles at the whole reservation. A language-model attempt goes out once (`max_attempts=1`) and is\n    12\tsent again, up to three attempts with production's pauses, when the service gave no answer: no HTTP status, or 429 or 5xx, as\n    13\t`model.openai_transport` retries; a client HTTP error is not. A response settles at its usage (the formula of `model.call`); one with no\n    14\tusage, and every failed attempt, at its reservation. An attempt whose reservation would pass the ceiling is not sent: it raises\n    15\t`model.TransportError`, and the production code records that item or judgement as failed. A response that costs more than its\n    16\treservation sets an overrun flag that refuses every later attempt of every run.\n    17\t\n    18\t  python3 budget_replay.py --ledger L --from D --until D --views V --archive A [--config C] [--only NVDA] [--workers 16] [--cap X]\n    19\t  python3 budget_replay.py --init-ledger L --ceiling 12.25\n    20\t\"\"\"\n    21\timport argparse\n    22\timport contextlib\n    23\timport fcntl\n    24\timport io\n    25\timport json\n    26\timport math\n    27\timport os\n    28\timport sys\n    29\timport threading\n    30\timport time\n    31\timport types\n    32\timport urllib.error\n    33\timport urllib.request\n    34\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    35\t\n    36\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    37\tROOT = HERE.parents[1]\n    38\tsys.path.insert(0, str(ROOT / 'src'))\n    39\tfrom hivemind import config, jev, model, parallel_day, replay, universe  # noqa: E402\n    40\t\n    41\tCLIENT_ERRORS = {400, 401, 403, 404}\n    42\t# How many reservations the calling thread holds and has not settled (the interception proofs read it). One object per process, kept in\n    43\t# sys.modules, because each component loads this file under its own module object.\n    44\t_MARK = sys.modules.setdefault('hivemind_shared_ledger_live_mark', types.ModuleType('hivemind_shared_ledger_live_mark'))\n    45\tif not hasattr(_MARK, 'local'):\n    46\t    _MARK.local = threading.local()\n    47\t_LIVE = _MARK.local\n    48\t\n    49\t\n    50\tdef holds_live_reservation():\n    51\t    \"\"\"Whether the calling thread holds a reservation it has not yet settled (WIRING-DESIGN.md §B2, the interception trap).\"\"\"\n    52\t    return getattr(_LIVE, 'depth', 0) > 0\n    53\t\n    54\t\n    55\tdef lock_is_free(lock_path):\n    56\t    \"\"\"Whether no process holds the lock file: a shared, non-blocking flock succeeds only when no exclusive holder is alive.\"\"\"\n    57\t    try:\n    58\t        with open(lock_path, 'a') as handle:\n    59\t            try:\n    60\t                fcntl.flock(handle, fcntl.LOCK_SH | fcntl.LOCK_NB)\n    61\t            except BlockingIOError:\n    62\t                return False\n    63\t            fcntl.flock(handle, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n    64\t            return True\n    65\t    except OSError:\n    66\t        return True\n    67\t\n    68\t\n    69\tdef install_stop_gate(ledger):\n    70\t    \"\"\"Process-wide: every `urllib.request.urlopen` call, a helper's internal retry included, first reads the shared ledger. When it is\n    71\t    stopped, or when a registered launcher's lock is free (that launcher died without removing its entry: the gate then stops the\n    72\t    ledger), the call is refused as HTTP 403 (no component retries a 403) and nothing is sent. An attempt whose read came before a stop\n    73\t    was written may finish; one that reads after it is refused. Returns a function that removes the gate.\"\"\"\n    74\t    original = urllib.request.urlopen\n    75\t\n    76\t    def gated(request, *arguments, **keywords):\n    77\t        state = ledger.stop_if_a_launcher_is_gone()\n    78\t        if state.get('stopped'):\n    79\t            raise urllib.error.HTTPError(getattr(request, 'full_url', str(request)), 403,\n    80\t                                         f\"the shared ledger is stopped ({state.get('stop_reason')}): not sent\", {},\n    81\t                                         io.BytesIO(b'{\"error\": {\"type\": \"shared_ledger_stopped\"}}'))\n    82\t        return original(request, *arguments, **keywords)\n    83\t    urllib.request.urlopen = gated\n    84\t\n    85\t    def remove():\n    86\t        if urllib.request.urlopen is gated:\n    87\t            urllib.request.urlopen = original\n    88\t    return remove\n    89\t\n    90\t\n    91\tclass SharedLedger:\n    92\t    \"\"\"One JSON ledger for every run under a ceiling, guarded by an exclusive file lock (processes and threads). The first refused\n    93\t    reservation, or an explicit `stop`, persists `stopped` with its reason under the lock: every later reservation, in any thread or\n    94\t    process, is refused (WIRING-DESIGN.md §B2, D448).\"\"\"\n    95\t\n    96\t    def __init__(self, path):\n    97\t        self.path = Path(path)\n    98\t        self.lock_path = self.path.with_suffix('.lock')\n    99\t        self.attempts_path = self.path.with_suffix('.attempts.jsonl')   # one line per settled or refused attempt: the measurement record\n   100\t\n   101\t    def _note(self, entry):\n   102\t        \"\"\"Append one attempt line (the process, the call kind, the amounts, the tokens when reported, the moment) for per-component\n   103\t        spend and timing. Written only after the ledger's state is saved, and best-effort: a failed append never blocks a stop, a charge\n   104\t        or an overrun. A crash between the two writes leaves the lines short of the ledger, never ahead of it; the tally reports the gap\n   105\t        as unaccounted. One short append, so concurrent lines do not interleave.\"\"\"\n   106\t        line = json.dumps(dict(entry, pid=os.getpid(), at=time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z'))) + '\\n'\n   107\t        try:\n   108\t            with open(self.attempts_path, 'a') as handle:\n   109\t                handle.write(line)\n   110\t        except OSError:\n   111\t            pass\n   112\t\n   113\t    @contextlib.contextmanager\n   114\t    def _locked(self):\n   115\t        with open(self.lock_path, 'a+') as lock:\n   116\t            fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX)\n   117\t            try:\n   118\t                state = json.loads(self.path.read_text())\n   119\t                yield state\n   120\t                temporary = self.path.with_suffix('.tmp')\n   121\t                temporary.write_text(json.dumps(state, indent=1))\n   122\t                os.replace(temporary, self.path)          # a reader never sees a half-written ledger\n   123\t            finally:\n   124\t                fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n   125\t\n   126\t    @staticmethod\n   127\t    def init(path, ceiling):\n   128\t        Path(path).write_text(json.dumps({'ceiling': ceiling, 'spent': 0.0, 'reserved': 0.0, 'overrun': False, 'refused': 0, 'attempts': 0,\n   129\t                                          'stopped': False, 'stop_reason': None}, indent=1))\n   130\t\n   131\t    def reserve(self, amount):\n   132\t        with self._locked() as state:\n   133\t            granted = not (state.get('stopped') or state['overrun'] or state['spent'] + state['reserved'] + amount > state['ceiling'])\n   134\t            if granted:\n   135\t                state['reserved'] += amount\n   136\t                state['attempts'] += 1\n   137\t            else:\n   138\t                state['refused'] += 1\n   139\t                if not state.get('stopped'):\n   140\t                    state['stopped'] = True\n   141\t                    state['stop_reason'] = 'overrun' if state['overrun'] else f'a reservation of ${amount:.6f} would pass the ${state[\"ceiling\"]:.2f} ceiling'\n   142\t        if not granted:\n   143\t            self._note({'refused': amount})                  # after the stop is saved\n   144\t            return False\n   145\t        _LIVE.depth = getattr(_LIVE, 'depth', 0) + 1\n   146\t        return True\n   147\t\n   148\t    @staticmethod\n   149\t    def estimate(request):\n   150\t        \"\"\"The shared reservation of one language-model attempt (WIRING-DESIGN.md §B): `llm_estimate`.\"\"\"\n   151\t        return llm_estimate(request)\n   152\t\n   153\t    def settle(self, reserved, actual, kind='llm', tokens=None):\n   154\t        \"\"\"Settle one attempt. A missing, non-finite, zero or negative charge settles at the whole reservation: an answered paid\n   155\t        request never costs nothing, and a failed attempt is charged its reservation. `kind` (llm, jev, embeddings) and the reported\n   156\t        `tokens` go to the attempt line only.\"\"\"\n   157\t        _LIVE.depth = max(0, getattr(_LIVE, 'depth', 0) - 1)\n   158\t        if not isinstance(actual, (int, float)) or isinstance(actual, bool) or not math.isfinite(actual) or actual <= 0:\n   159\t            actual = reserved\n   160\t        with self._locked() as state:\n   161\t            state['reserved'] = max(0.0, state['reserved'] - reserved)\n   162\t            state['spent'] += actual\n   163\t            if actual > reserved + 1e-12:\n   164\t                state['overrun'] = True\n   165\t                if not state.get('stopped'):\n   166\t                    state['stopped'], state['stop_reason'] = True, 'overrun'\n   167\t        self._note({'kind': kind, 'reserved': reserved, 'charged': actual, **({'tokens': tokens} if tokens is not None else {})})\n   168\t\n   169\t    def stop_if_a_launcher_is_gone(self):\n   170\t        \"\"\"Under the ledger's lock: a registered launcher whose lock no process holds died without removing its entry; the ledger is\n   171\t        stopped. A relaunch calls this before it takes its own lock, so its new lock never makes a stale entry look alive; the stop gate\n   172\t        calls it before every send. Returns the state after the check.\"\"\"\n   173\t        with self._locked() as state:\n   174\t            if not state.get('stopped'):\n   175\t                for label, lock_path in sorted((state.get('launchers') or {}).items()):\n   176\t                    if lock_is_free(lock_path):\n   177\t                        state['stopped'], state['stop_reason'] = True, f'the launcher of {label} is gone'\n   178\t                        break\n   179\t            return dict(state)\n   180\t\n   181\t    def claim_launcher(self, label, lock_handle):\n   182\t        \"\"\"One step under the ledger's lock (council mo, D449): stop the ledger if any registered launcher's lock is free; try the\n   183\t        launcher's own lock without blocking (False: another launch of this label is running); if this label is still registered once\n   184\t        its lock is held, the previous launcher died without removing its entry, so the ledger stops; then register. The stop gate's\n   185\t        probe takes the same lock, so no send can see a stale entry as alive while a new launcher takes its lock.\"\"\"\n   186\t        with self._locked() as state:\n   187\t            launchers = state.setdefault('launchers', {})\n   188\t            if not state.get('stopped'):\n   189\t                for other, lock_path in sorted(launchers.items()):\n   190\t                    if lock_is_free(lock_path):\n   191\t                        state['stopped'], state['stop_reason'] = True, f'the launcher of {other} is gone'\n   192\t                        break\n   193\t            try:\n   194\t                fcntl.flock(lock_handle, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)\n   195\t            except BlockingIOError:\n   196\t                return False\n   197\t            if label in launchers and not state.get('stopped'):\n   198\t                state['stopped'], state['stop_reason'] = True, f'the launcher of {label} is gone'\n   199\t            launchers[label] = os.path.abspath(lock_handle.name)\n   200\t            return True\n   201\t\n   202\t    def register_launcher(self, label, lock_path):\n   203\t        \"\"\"Register a launcher's lock path (tests and proofs; a launcher uses `claim_launcher`).\"\"\"\n   204\t        with self._locked() as state:\n   205\t            state.setdefault('launchers', {})[label] = str(lock_path)\n   206\t\n   207\t    def remove_launcher(self, label):\n   208\t        \"\"\"Called by a launcher on a normal end, before it releases its lock.\"\"\"\n   209\t        with self._locked() as state:\n   210\t            (state.get('launchers') or {}).pop(label, None)\n   211\t\n   212\t    def stop(self, reason):\n   213\t        \"\"\"A stop any component records: every later reservation is refused.\"\"\"\n   214\t        with self._locked() as state:\n   215\t            if not state.get('stopped'):\n   216\t                state['stopped'], state['stop_reason'] = True, reason\n   217\t\n   218\t    def read(self):\n   219\t        with open(self.lock_path, 'a+') as lock:\n   220\t            fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX)\n   221\t            try:\n   222\t                return json.loads(self.path.read_text())\n   223\t            finally:\n   224\t                fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n   225\t\n   226\t\n   227\tdef llm_estimate(request):\n   228\t    price_in, price_out = model.PRICES[request['model']]\n   229\t    cap = request.get('max_completion_tokens', request.get('max_tokens'))\n   230\t    if isinstance(cap, bool) or not isinstance(cap, int) or cap <= 0:\n   231\t        raise model.TransportError('request without a positive output cap: refused by the budget')\n   232\t    tokens_in = 2 * len(json.dumps(request).encode('utf-8')) + 4096\n   233\t    return (tokens_in * price_in + cap * price_out) / 1e6\n   234\t\n   235\t\n   236\tdef whole(count):\n   237\t    return isinstance(count, int) and not isinstance(count, bool) and count >= 0\n   238\t\n   239\t\n   240\tdef llm_cost(response, request):\n   241\t    \"\"\"The answer's cost by `model.call`'s formula; None (settled at the reservation) unless the counts are whole, nonnegative numbers\n   242\t    with some input.\"\"\"\n   243\t    usage = response.get('usage') if isinstance(response, dict) else None\n   244\t    if not isinstance(usage, dict):\n   245\t        return None\n   246\t    tokens_in, tokens_out = usage.get('prompt_tokens'), usage.get('completion_tokens')\n   247\t    details = usage.get('prompt_tokens_details') or {}\n   248\t    cached = (details.get('cached_tokens') or 0) if isinstance(details, dict) else None\n   249\t    if not (whole(tokens_in) and whole(tokens_out) and whole(cached)) or tokens_in == 0:\n   250\t        return None\n   251\t    price_in, price_out = model.PRICES[request['model']]\n   252\t    cached = min(tokens_in, cached)\n   253\t    return (tokens_in - cached) / 1e6 * price_in + cached / 1e6 * price_in * model.CACHED_INPUT_SHARE + tokens_out / 1e6 * price_out\n   254\t\n   255\t\n   256\tdef jev_estimate(request):\n   257\t    price_in, _ = model.PRICES[request['model']]\n   258\t    tokens_in = 2 * len(json.dumps(request, sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False).encode('utf-8')) + 4096\n   259\t    return jev.ATTEMPTS * tokens_in * price_in / 1e6\n   260\t\n   261\t\n   262\tclass BudgetTransport:\n   263\t    \"\"\"Called as production calls a transport: (provider, request) for a language model, (request,) for Jev.\"\"\"\n   264\t\n   265\t    def __init__(self, ledger, sleep=time.sleep):\n   266\t        self.ledger, self.sleep = ledger, sleep\n   267\t\n   268\t    def __call__(self, *args):\n   269\t        if len(args) == 1:\n   270\t            return self._jev(args[0])\n   271\t        provider, request = args\n   272\t        for attempt in range(3):\n   273\t            estimate = llm_estimate(request)\n   274\t            if not self.ledger.reserve(estimate):\n   275\t                raise model.TransportError('the shared budget ceiling would be passed: not sent')\n   276\t            try:\n   277\t                response = model.openai_transport(provider, request, max_attempts=1)\n   278\t            except model.TransportError as error:\n   279\t                self.ledger.settle(estimate, estimate)\n   280\t                status = getattr(error, 'http_status', None)\n   281\t                if status in CLIENT_ERRORS or attempt == 2 or (status is not None and status != 429 and status < 500):\n   282\t                    raise\n   283\t                self.sleep(5 * (attempt + 1))\n   284\t                continue\n   285\t            except Exception:\n   286\t                self.ledger.settle(estimate, estimate)\n   287\t                raise\n   288\t            cost = llm_cost(response, request)\n   289\t            self.ledger.settle(estimate, estimate if cost is None else cost)\n   290\t            return response\n   291\t        raise model.TransportError('no answer after three attempts')\n   292\t\n   293\t    def _jev(self, request):\n   294\t        estimate = jev_estimate(request)\n   295\t        if not self.ledger.reserve(estimate):\n   296\t            raise model.TransportError('the shared budget ceiling would be passed: not sent')\n   297\t        try:\n   298\t            response = jev.transport(request)\n   299\t        except Exception:\n   300\t            self.ledger.settle(estimate, estimate, kind='jev')\n   301\t            raise\n   302\t        usage = response.get('usage') if isinstance(response, dict) else None\n   303\t        tokens = usage.get('input_tokens') if isinstance(usage, dict) else None\n   304\t        if isinstance(tokens, int) and not isinstance(tokens, bool) and tokens > 0:\n   305\t            # an unanswered attempt inside jev.transport was the same request, so each of the three is billed at most this input;\n   306\t            # the whole charge is settled, so a charge above the reservation sets the overrun flag\n   307\t            actual = jev.ATTEMPTS * tokens * model.PRICES[request['model']][0] / 1e6\n   308\t        else:\n   309\t            actual = estimate                       # usage missing, invalid, or zero for a nonempty request: the whole reservation\n   310\t        self.ledger.settle(estimate, actual, kind='jev', tokens=tokens if isinstance(tokens, int) else None)\n   311\t        return response\n   312\t\n   313\t\n   314\tdef main():\n   315\t    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n   316\t    parser.add_argument('--init-ledger')\n   317\t    parser.add_argument('--ceiling', type=float)\n   318\t    parser.add_argument('--ledger')\n   319\t    parser.add_argument('--from', dest='from_day')\n   320\t    parser.add_argument('--until', dest='until_day')\n   321\t    parser.add_argument('--testdb', default=str(ROOT / 'poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite'))\n   322\t    parser.add_argument('--views')\n   323\t    parser.add_argument('--archive')\n   324\t    parser.add_argument('--config', default=str(ROOT / 'poc/config/poc-v118.json'))\n   325\t    parser.add_argument('--universe', default=str(ROOT / 'poc/universe.json'))\n   326\t    parser.add_argument('--only', default='NVDA')\n   327\t    parser.add_argument('--cap', type=float, default=None)\n   328\t    parser.add_argument('--workers', type=int, default=16)\n   329\t    parser.add_argument('--jev-workers', type=int, default=None)\n   330\t    args = parser.parse_args()\n   331\t    if args.init_ledger:\n   332\t        if Path(args.init_ledger).exists():\n   333\t            raise SystemExit('ledger exists: refused (a new ledger needs a new path)')\n   334\t        SharedLedger.init(args.init_ledger, args.ceiling)\n   335\t        print('ledger written:', args.init_ledger, 'ceiling', args.ceiling)\n   336\t        return\n   337\t    for name in ('ledger', 'from_day', 'until_day', 'views', 'archive'):\n   338\t        if not getattr(args, name):\n   339\t            raise SystemExit(f'--{name.replace(\"_day\", \"\")} is required')\n   340\t    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(args.views), exist_ok=True)\n   341\t    log_path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(args.views), 'replay.log')\n   342\t\n   343\t    def log(*parts):\n   344\t        line = ' '.join(str(p) for p in parts)\n   345\t        print(line, flush=True)\n   346\t        with open(log_path, 'a', encoding='utf-8') as handle:\n   347\t            handle.write(line + '\\n')\n   348\t\n   349\t    cfg = config.load(args.config)\n   350\t    if cfg.baseline is not None:\n   351\t        raise SystemExit(f'config {cfg.version} uses the retired seeded-question baseline: refused')\n   352\t    import importlib.util\n   353\t    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('scripts_replay', ROOT / 'scripts/replay.py')\n   354\t    scripts_replay = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n   355\t    spec.loader.exec_module(scripts_replay)\n   356\t    scripts_replay.refuse_links_engine(cfg)                   # the same refusals scripts/replay.py makes\n   357\t    scripts_replay.refuse_seeded_ledger(args.views)\n   358\t    scripts_replay.refuse_harness_ledger(args.views)\n   359\t    uni = universe.load(args.universe)\n   360\t    archive = model.Archive(args.archive)\n   361\t    ledger = SharedLedger(args.ledger)\n   362\t    install_stop_gate(ledger)                              # Jev's internal retries read the run's stop too (council mo R1)\n   363\t    transport = BudgetTransport(ledger)\n   364\t    jev_workers = args.jev_workers if args.jev_workers is not None else (8 if cfg.matching.engine == 'jev' else 1)\n   365\t    day_runner = parallel_day.ParallelDay(os.path.abspath(args.testdb), os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(args.views)), 'manifests'),\n   366\t                                          extraction_workers=args.workers, jev_workers=jev_workers) if args.workers > 1 else None\n   367\t    log(f'--- budgeted replay {args.from_day}..{args.until_day} config {cfg.version}/{cfg.checksum} provider {cfg.provider} universe {uni.version} '\n   368\t        f'workers {args.workers}; ledger {args.ledger} {ledger.read()}')\n   369\t    totals = replay.run(testdb_path=args.testdb, views_path=args.views, archive=archive, cfg=cfg, universe=uni,\n   370\t                        from_day=args.from_day, until_day=args.until_day, spend_cap_usd=args.cap, log=log,\n   371\t                        only_objects=set(args.only.split(',')) if args.only else None, transport=transport, day_runner=day_runner)\n   372\t    log('totals:', totals)\n   373\t    log('ledger:', ledger.read())\n   374\t\n   375\t\n   376\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   377\t    main()\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
